The MSE policy outputs \-0\.001: go straight, the average of two groups that lean opposite ways. The binned policy puts 0\.48 of its probability on steering left and 0\.51 on steering right, and taking the most likely bin commits it to one side. One step later the robot is no longer at $y = 0$, the demos near its new position all agree, and the choice sticks.

